#### A PA who moved fast, a doctor who wasn't worried, and a pathology report that had something to say about both of those calls.

> *Originally published on Substack. Imported to My Ledger as part of the adrenal journey series.*

Quick recap if you're just joining. [Chapter 1](https://v64otd.com/ledger/my-adrenal-tumors-journey-chapter-1/) covered seventeen years of diagnoses that turned out to share one cause nobody connected. [Chapter 2](https://v64otd.com/ledger/my-adrenal-tumors-journey-chapter-2/) covered the diagnosis itself: masses on both adrenal glands, Cushing's confirmed, and a multidisciplinary team at UT Southwestern reviewing my case together. [Chapter 3](https://v64otd.com/ledger/my-adrenal-tumors-journey-chapter-3/) covered a GI visit where I brought six pages of prepared questions and left with most of them unanswered, a set of labs nobody walked me through, and a voicemail recommending an upper endoscopy that showed up at almost the same time as my Google review.

This chapter is about what happened when I finally got that endoscopy, and what it found.

##### Why was I there in the first place

I want to clear up something the chapter order might hide. I didn't go to my GI practice because of the Cushing's diagnosis. I went because my iron and B12 were low and weren't coming up. Both were documented in my labs, and I wanted a GI specialist's opinion, because absorption problems that won't go away belong in that specialty's lane.

That didn't go the way I expected. The message I came away with from that first appointment was that there wasn't really a problem worth chasing yet. The iron deficiency wasn't bad enough to act on. The B12 wasn't alarming. Unless I had symptoms, clear and obvious ones, the plan was basically to wait and watch, with nothing to watch.

I brought more than lab numbers. I'm also diabetic and on Mounjaro, a GLP-1 drug (technically a dual GIP/GLP-1 agonist) that's known to slow down how fast the stomach empties. That's part of how it works. So I had a real question. Could that slower emptying, combined with whatever was going on with my stomach acid, be setting up an absorption problem that explained why oral supplements weren't moving my numbers? That seemed like exactly the kind of drug, condition and lab question a GI specialist would want to think through. It didn't get much traction.

I also brought up one thing I thought might count as a symptom: an occasional pain up under my left rib cage, mostly after a big or heavy meal. Mexican food was the reliable example. Not constant, not severe, but there often enough that I'd noticed the pattern.

And I asked, straight out, for an upper endoscopy. The answer was no. Not indicated. Not necessary at this point.

Almost exactly fifteen minutes in, the PA stood up. The visit was over. Along with the blood work from Chapter 3, I left with an order for an abdominal ultrasound. That ultrasound came back with findings the June CT never showed. The June CT reported the liver as homogeneous throughout, with no focal lesions. The September ultrasound showed something different: **fatty infiltration of the liver; a 1.6 x 2.0 x 1.6-centimeter hypoechoic mass in the left lobe, described as non-cystic and possibly a hemangioma or other mass; and an enlarged spleen.**

I wasn't expecting liver findings. That's worth saying plainly, because it sums up how this whole thing has gone. I came in about iron and B12 and left with a liver that needs a second look. There's no formal diagnosis yet, but the picture is trending toward NASH, non-alcoholic steatohepatitis. That's liver disease driven by fat buildup and inflammation, not alcohol. I don't drink. What I do have is years of uncontrolled hypercortisolism, which is a well-documented driver of fatty liver disease through what it does to fat metabolism, insulin resistance and the deep belly fat around the organs. One more system. One more thread leading back to the same place.

##### The callback

Two days later, I got a voicemail from the practice. The PA had spoken with the doctor directly, and the doctor agreed: an upper endoscopy was now recommended after all.

That deserves an honest look, not a neat story.

One explanation is simple and generous. The PA presented my case to the doctor, the doctor looked at it with fresh eyes, and a more experienced clinician decided the scope was warranted. That's exactly how the system is supposed to work, and if that's what happened, it worked. I'm glad it did.

The other explanation is harder to rule out, and anyone who's spent time dealing with American health care will recognize it. An endoscopy is a billable procedure. A patient who leaves unhappy and writes about it is a risk. By the time of that call, I had already left the Google review from Chapter 3. Whether anyone at the practice had seen it yet, I don't know. I don't have enough information to draw a conclusion, and I'm not going to pretend I do.

So I'm right where I was in Chapter 3. Both explanations are possible. I'm noting the order things happened in, not accusing anyone.

##### Getting to the room

Once the endoscopy was scheduled, I prepared the same way I have for every big appointment in this process: a written summary of my symptoms, my relevant history and the specific questions I needed answered. The pre-procedure appointment covered the scope logistics and moved quickly through the clinical checklist. The bigger question I kept trying to raise, whether something in my stomach might be explaining more than one problem at once, kept falling on deaf ears.

I went into the endoscopy without much reassurance. That turned out to be the right attitude.

##### What they found

The pathology report came back with findings specific enough to belong in this series. Not because I want to turn a medical record into a grievance, but because the tissue showed exactly what my questions had been aimed at all along.

The report documented **ECL cell hyperplasia, focal mucosal atrophy, and focal intestinal metaplasia.**

If those terms are new to you, they were new to me too until recently. Here's what they mean in plain language.

**ECL cell hyperplasia** is an overgrowth of a certain type of cell in the stomach lining. These cells make histamine, which triggers acid production. When they multiply abnormally, it's usually because the stomach isn't making enough acid on its own, and the body is pushing the system harder to make up for it. It's also a known precursor to a type of gastric carcinoid tumor, so it gets watched closely once it's found.

**Focal mucosal atrophy** means parts of the stomach lining have thinned out and lost working cells, specifically parietal cells. Those cells make stomach acid and intrinsic factor. Intrinsic factor is what lets your body absorb B12. No working intrinsic factor, no B12 absorption, no matter how much you take by mouth. And without enough stomach acid, iron doesn't absorb well either. This finding, next to months of oral supplements that barely moved my iron and B12 numbers, changed the question I'd been asking since the very first appointment.

**Focal intestinal metaplasia** means parts of the stomach lining have been replaced by cells that look more like intestinal tissue than stomach tissue. It's a precancerous change that needs ongoing surveillance. Showing up alongside the other two findings, it points to a pattern, not a one-off.

Put together, the working picture from these findings has a name: **Autoimmune Metaplastic Atrophic Gastritis. AMAG.** It's an autoimmune condition in which the immune system attacks the stomach's parietal cells, gradually breaking down the stomach lining. If that's what this is, it would explain the B12 problem. It would explain why oral iron wasn't moving the needle on my numbers. It may even explain the pain under my left rib cage after a heavy meal, since a stomach with low acid production and a damaged lining doesn't handle large meals the way a healthy one does. And it puts the Mounjaro question I raised at the start in a new light. The slower emptying I was asking about wasn't the whole story, but it was pointing in the right direction. My stomach wasn't working right. The question was always worth asking.

The reason I walked into that practice in the first place was iron and B12 that weren't coming up. The pathology report pointed to a likely explanation for both. It just took a scope and a biopsy to find it, and a callback two days after I was turned down to get the scope scheduled in the first place.

##### The part that keeps coming back to me

I want to be fair here, because this series has tried to be fair the whole way. The endoscopy was ordered, it happened, and these findings exist because someone made that call. That matters.

But I keep coming back to that appointment that ended at fifteen minutes. The iron and B12 questions that weren't worth chasing. The Mounjaro question that didn't get traction. The rib cage pain that didn't move the needle. The endoscopy request that was turned down, then reversed 48 hours later, after a conversation I wasn't part of.

I'm not drawing a straight line from any of that to what the pathology report said. I'm not qualified to make that call. What I'm saying is that the questions were right, the symptom was real, the request was reasonable, and the answer was still no. Until, for whatever reason, it wasn't.

You can decide what that sequence means. I've stopped being surprised by it.

##### Where this fits in the larger picture

AMAG is an autoimmune condition. I now have documented autoimmune activity in my stomach, elevated TPO antibodies that suggest an autoimmune tendency at the thyroid, hip arthritis in a joint that was never injured, and years of uncontrolled hypercortisolism under all of it.

Long-term cortisol excess affects the immune system in a specific way. It suppresses some immune pathways and throws others off balance. It also directly damages the gut's protective lining, exposing the immune system to things it wouldn't normally encounter and triggering autoimmune responses that might otherwise remain quiet. I'm not offering my case as proof of that. I'm saying the mechanism exists, it's documented, and my case is turning into a pretty thorough example of what it looks like when nobody steps back far enough to see the whole picture until year seventeen.

The AVS is on October 26th. On October 12th, I have a follow-up to go over the endoscopy findings in detail and set the GI plan from here. There are surveillance intervals to set, antibody panels that haven't been ordered yet, and at least one referral conversation I plan to have that hasn't happened yet.

There's a bigger failure worth naming, because this series has been circling it for four chapters without saying it outright. The specialists in my care are, one by one, competent people doing their jobs in their own lanes. That's exactly the problem. My endocrinologist handles the adrenals. My GI practice handles the digestive tract. Nobody's job description includes stepping back far enough to see that years of excess cortisol damage the gut lining, throw the immune system off, cause fatty liver disease, hurt the absorption of the very nutrients that keep everything else running, and put fat in places that cause their own problems. And that all of it is connected, active, and relevant to each other right now. None of them created that gap. The system created it by design, then handed it to the patient to manage alone.

One more thing I've been chewing on: I'm not sure what my primary care doctor is for anymore. I don't need referrals. My specialists refer to each other, when they refer at all. What I actually need is someone whose job is to hold the whole picture at once. Someone who looks at an endocrinology finding and asks what it means for GI. Who looks at a GI finding and asks what it means for absorption and surgery planning. Who looks at a liver finding and asks what it means for everything else already in motion. And then takes one more step and asks: given all of this, what else might be going on that nobody has looked for yet? That person should exist. In a system that works, that person is the primary care doctor. What I have instead is a group of specialists who each own one piece, stay inside it, and hand the patient back with a follow-up scheduled. Nobody asks the next question. The job exists. Nobody's in it.

I'm not a doctor. I shouldn't be the one holding all of this together. I'm doing it anyway, because otherwise nobody does.

I'm still the one with all three specialists' calendars open at once. What still catches me off guard, even now, is opening a pathology report and finding out the questions I wrote down on a piece of paper were the right ones all along.

Chapter 5 comes when October 12th, the FibroScan, the AVS, or whatever follows gives me something worth writing about. Probably sooner than I'd like.

> **Update:** The October 16th FibroScan never happened. I showed up without fasting, which it turns out I was supposed to do. That instruction wasn't in my appointment notes on the patient portal. It was only in a text message reminder I got about five days before. I hate text messages for this. There's so much spam now that the real ones get buried, and there's no reason instructions like that can't live in the calendar appointment and the portal reminders, where patients actually look. It hasn't been rescheduled yet. More in Chapter 5.
